<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kama River</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kama is the largest left-bank tributary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a>, about 1,800 km long, rising in the uplands of Udmurtia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and flowing past Perm to join the Volga below <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a>. The land around its confluence with the Volga was the heartland of Volga <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, and its upper basin, the land of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/komi-permyaks">Komi-Permyaks</a>, was long a source of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a> for Novgorod and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>.</p></div>
